## tree-sitter grammars - vendored definition-capture queries

**Files:** `src/MeisterDev.ProPR.CodeAnalysis.TreeSitter/Queries/*/tags.scm` (embedded into the assembly)
**Licence:** MIT, per grammar:

| Query file | Upstream grammar | Copyright |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `go/tags.scm` | https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-go | `Copyright (c) 2014 Max Brunsfeld` |
| `java/tags.scm` | https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-java | `Copyright (c) 2017 Ayman Nadeem` |
| `javascript/tags.scm` | https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-javascript | `Copyright (c) 2014 Max Brunsfeld` |
| `python/tags.scm` | https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-python | `Copyright (c) 2016 Max Brunsfeld` |
| `ruby/tags.scm` | https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-ruby | `Copyright (c) 2016 Rob Rix` |
| `typescript/tags.scm`, `tsx/tags.scm` | https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-typescript | `Copyright (c) 2017 Max Brunsfeld` |

Each file is adapted from that grammar's own `queries/tags.scm`: the definition captures are kept and the
doc-comment predicates are dropped, because the symbol-prefetch path needs only a definition node and its name.
The grammars themselves are not vendored - they arrive through the `TreeSitter.DotNet` package at build time.

